1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,975
Pick something you've genuinely considered automating with an agent, or the refund

2
00:00:04,975 --> 00:00:08,754
case this section keeps returning to if nothing else comes to mind.

3
00:00:08,754 --> 00:00:13,323
Open A08, and give this the same twenty minutes every workshop in this course has

4
00:00:13,323 --> 00:00:13,887
asked for.

5
00:00:13,887 --> 00:00:15,297
The bar is identical too.

6
00:00:15,297 --> 00:00:15,974
Not perfect.

7
00:00:15,974 --> 00:00:16,482
Complete.

8
00:00:16,482 --> 00:00:21,220
Every question answered with something, even where that something is a number you'll

9
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:25,845
validate later, the same move as Section three and Section five's workshops before

10
00:00:25,845 --> 00:00:26,353
this one.

11
00:00:26,353 --> 00:00:30,809
Automation is the right thing to practise scoping on precisely because it's the

12
00:00:30,809 --> 00:00:35,152
branch of Section two's decision tree with the most ways to go wrong quietly.

13
00:00:35,152 --> 00:00:37,521
Retrieval fails by returning a bad answer.

14
00:00:37,521 --> 00:00:40,003
An agent that's scoped badly can act on one.

15
00:00:40,553 --> 00:00:45,070
Notice minutes four through eight ask you to actually compute 6.3's number, not

16
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:46,443
estimate it from memory.

17
00:00:46,443 --> 00:00:51,132
Count the real steps in your chain, pick a defensible per-step accuracy, and raise

18
00:00:51,132 --> 00:00:52,105
it to that power.

19
00:00:52,105 --> 00:00:56,050
Most people find this the slowest four minutes and the most valuable.

20
00:00:56,050 --> 00:00:58,910
Why does the blast radius block get the most time?

21
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:03,656
Because sorting every single action into last lecture's four rows, not just the one

22
00:01:03,656 --> 00:01:08,231
you already suspected was risky, is usually where a workshop like this finds the

23
00:01:08,231 --> 00:01:11,320
thing worth actually changing before anyone builds it.

24
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:16,123
The middle block, minutes eight through twelve, goes quickly for most proposals, and

25
00:01:16,123 --> 00:01:16,810
that's fine.

26
00:01:16,810 --> 00:01:21,442
Plan-then-execute is usually the obvious right call once you say it out loud, and

27
00:01:21,442 --> 00:01:25,959
most tasks turn out not to need a second agent at all once 6.5's question, what

28
00:01:25,959 --> 00:01:30,477
specifically would one agent fail to do, gets asked directly instead of assumed

29
00:01:30,477 --> 00:01:30,763
away.

30
00:01:30,763 --> 00:01:35,338
The last three minutes are a single pass, not a new topic: run your own proposal

31
00:01:35,338 --> 00:01:39,970
against all four of last lecture's disqualifying rows, out loud, with the numbers

32
00:01:39,970 --> 00:01:42,487
you just computed rather than a fresh guess.

33
00:01:43,037 --> 00:01:44,240
Recognise this shape.

34
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:48,998
It's last lecture's good column, and it's roughly what your own worksheet's summary

35
00:01:48,998 --> 00:01:53,412
should sound like once minutes zero through seventeen are actually filled in.

36
00:01:53,412 --> 00:01:57,826
Notice everything this sentence specifies: the scope, the bound, the cap, and

37
00:01:57,826 --> 00:02:00,004
exactly who gets the cases outside it.

38
00:02:00,004 --> 00:02:04,188
Notice too what it doesn't specify: no tool name, no framework, no model.

39
00:02:04,188 --> 00:02:08,774
That's the same boundary Section three drew around the PRD and Section five drew

40
00:02:08,774 --> 00:02:10,265
around the RAG design doc.

41
00:02:10,265 --> 00:02:14,621
This document says what the system must do and where a human stays involved.

42
00:02:14,621 --> 00:02:17,258
How to build it stays an engineering decision.

43
00:02:17,258 --> 00:02:21,557
Compare it against whatever you'd have written before this section started.

44
00:02:21,557 --> 00:02:26,028
Most first attempts at describing an automation idea sound like "let the agent

45
00:02:26,028 --> 00:02:30,556
handle refunds automatically," a sentence with nothing specific enough in it to

46
00:02:30,556 --> 00:02:32,505
actually scope, let alone approve.

47
00:02:32,505 --> 00:02:36,059
Twenty minutes from now, yours should read closer to this one.

48
00:02:36,609 --> 00:02:40,755
Say the honest caveat, because minute four is where people quietly stop being

49
00:02:40,755 --> 00:02:44,308
rigorous, exactly when rigour is the entire point of the exercise.

50
00:02:44,308 --> 00:02:48,830
If your honest chain length and a defensible per-step accuracy produce a whole-chain

51
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:52,168
number that makes you wince, that's not the worksheet failing.

52
00:02:52,168 --> 00:02:56,637
That's the worksheet doing its job four sections before launch instead of during an

53
00:02:56,637 --> 00:02:57,498
incident review.

54
00:02:57,498 --> 00:03:01,966
Write the real number down, then decide what to do about it: shorten the chain, add

55
00:03:01,966 --> 00:03:05,520
a checkpoint, or accept that this one stays manual a while longer.

56
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:09,234
The same discipline applies to the blast radius block right after it.

57
00:03:09,234 --> 00:03:13,703
It's tempting to sort a borderline action into the safer row because that's the row

58
00:03:13,703 --> 00:03:15,103
that lets you keep moving.

59
00:03:15,103 --> 00:03:16,448
Sort it honestly instead.

60
00:03:16,448 --> 00:03:20,809
The worksheet only protects you if the rows reflect the action as it actually is,

61
00:03:20,809 --> 00:03:22,747
not as you'd prefer it to be scored.

62
00:03:23,297 --> 00:03:27,953
That's today's bar, stated as plainly as it was in Section three and Section five:

63
00:03:27,953 --> 00:03:32,609
attempted, not perfected, and the one number that actually matters computed rather

64
00:03:32,609 --> 00:03:33,347
than guessed.

65
00:03:33,347 --> 00:03:36,185
If you finish with time left, don't polish evenly.

66
00:03:36,185 --> 00:03:40,727
Return to the blast radius section specifically, the same instruction as always,

67
00:03:40,727 --> 00:03:44,247
because it's the section most likely to be quietly incomplete.

68
00:03:44,247 --> 00:03:48,960
Resist the urge to smooth the reliability section instead, even though it's the one

69
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:50,890
with a satisfying number attached.

70
00:03:50,890 --> 00:03:55,546
A tidy number sitting next to an unsorted blast radius section is a worksheet that

71
00:03:55,546 --> 00:04:00,315
looks more finished than it is, and looking finished is exactly the trap this course

72
00:04:00,315 --> 00:04:01,905
keeps warning you away from.

73
00:04:02,455 --> 00:04:04,048
One thing before you move on.

74
00:04:04,048 --> 00:04:05,531
Finish all seven questions.

75
00:04:05,531 --> 00:04:10,091
Anywhere the honest answer is a guess, label it a guess rather than letting it read

76
00:04:10,091 --> 00:04:11,574
as more certain than it is.

77
00:04:11,574 --> 00:04:16,079
Then send it to whoever would actually build this, today, and ask which row they'd

78
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:16,958
challenge first.

79
00:04:16,958 --> 00:04:20,694
Expect it to be the blast radius section, not the reliability maths.

80
00:04:20,694 --> 00:04:22,892
Engineers usually trust your arithmetic.

81
00:04:22,892 --> 00:04:27,287
They want to know exactly who's approving what before they write a line of code.

82
00:04:27,287 --> 00:04:31,737
That question alone is often worth more than the twenty minutes you spent writing

83
00:04:31,737 --> 00:04:32,286
the draft.

84
00:04:32,286 --> 00:04:36,736
It's the same finding the support-corpus owner gave you back in Section five: the

85
00:04:36,736 --> 00:04:41,131
person closest to the system sees the gap in minutes that would have taken you a

86
00:04:41,131 --> 00:04:42,724
week to notice working alone.

87
00:04:42,724 --> 00:04:47,174
Next lecture closes this section: what you've actually built across ten lectures,

88
00:04:47,174 --> 00:04:51,789
and how directly it plugs into Section seven, where every one of these agents gets a

89
00:04:51,789 --> 00:04:54,261
real number attached to what it costs to run.
